<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
A name introduced by a <code>let</code>-binding, a <code>rec</code>-attrset
binding, an <code>inherit</code> clause, or a function parameter is never
used in its lexical scope. Unused bindings clutter the code, may indicate a
forgotten use site, and complicate downstream refactoring.
</p>
<p>
The deadnix-style convention applies: identifiers starting with an
underscore (e.g. <code>_module</code>, <code>_unused</code>) are
intentionally exempt. Use the <code>_</code>-prefix to signal that a
parameter is destructured for documentation or padding but not used.
</p>
<p>
The conventional <code>mkDerivation</code> callback-argument names
<code>finalAttrs</code>, <code>prevAttrs</code>, and <code>oldAttrs</code>
are also exempt — they may be declared for <code>overrideAttrs</code>
callers even when the package body itself does not reference them.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Remove the unused binding, or rename it with an underscore prefix to
acknowledge that it is intentionally unused.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>Unsafe — unused <code>let</code>-binding <code>x</code>:</p>
<sample language="nix">
let
  x = 1;
  y = 2;
in
y
</sample>
<p>Safe — both bindings are used:</p>
<sample language="nix">
let
  x = 1;
  y = 2;
in
x + y
</sample>
<p>Safe — intentionally-unused destructured parameter:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ _module, pkgs, ... }: pkgs
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>
<a href="https://github.com/astro/deadnix">deadnix</a>: a Nix dead-code
scanner whose algorithm and underscore-prefix convention this query follows.
</li>
<li>
CWE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/563.html">CWE-563:
Assignment to Variable without Use</a>
</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
